  "image_name": "f91982073a75c3bd2d6dfe1506dfa3a0.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how deactivation-enhanced RDRP can lead to two very different polymer architectures depending on the balance between chain growth and coupling. The scheme branches into a high \\(N_k\\) pathway, where intracyclization is favored, and a low \\(N_k\\) pathway, where oligomer combination dominates.\n\nIn the upper route, a linear primary chain carrying pendant multivinyl groups folds back on itself and reacts internally. This intrachain cyclization creates loops within the same molecule, eventually producing a single-chain-cyclized polymer. The final structure is shown as one convoluted chain containing several internal ring-like segments, highlighted as intrachain linkages.\n\nIn the lower route, short vinyl oligomers first form and then combine with one another through intermolecular reactions. These oligomeric species continue to add and combine further, building a much more connected structure. The end result is a hyperbranched polymer with many interchain linkages and a dense, irregular network-like morphology.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes that stronger internal cyclization yields isolated looped single chains, whereas greater oligomer–oligomer coupling drives formation of highly branched macromolecules. The legend distinguishes active or dormant species, multivinyl monomers, primary chains, and interchain versus intrachain linkages.",
